1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,127
In 2000, a small startup offered to sell itself to this company for $50 million.

2
00:00:06,127 --> 00:00:08,418
They laughed it out of the room.

3
00:00:08,418 --> 00:00:10,208
That startup was Netflix.

4
00:00:10,208 --> 00:00:13,788
This is the story of the $50-billion-dollar laugh.

5
00:00:14,388 --> 00:00:16,225
Blockbuster was everywhere.

6
00:00:16,225 --> 00:00:20,035
Over 9,000 stores, thousands of shelves of VHS and DVDs.

7
00:00:20,035 --> 00:00:24,663
Friday night didn't start until you walked through those blue doors.

8
00:00:25,263 --> 00:00:26,932
But the world was changing.

9
00:00:26,932 --> 00:00:31,013
People wanted movies without late fees, without leaving the couch.

10
00:00:31,013 --> 00:00:34,537
Netflix offered exactly that, by mail, then by streaming.

11
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:36,825
Blockbuster's answer? More late fees.

12
00:00:37,425 --> 00:00:39,667
They had the money to buy Netflix.

13
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:42,635
They had the brand to launch streaming first.

14
00:00:42,635 --> 00:00:45,405
Instead, they protected their store model.

15
00:00:45,405 --> 00:00:50,154
By the time they pivoted, the stores were the anchor dragging them down.

16
00:00:50,154 --> 00:00:52,463
In 2010, they filed for bankruptcy.

17
00:00:53,063 --> 00:00:56,375
Here's the truth: Blockbuster didn't lose to Netflix.

18
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:58,750
They lost to their own business model.

19
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:03,500
The late fees that made them rich were the same thing their customers hated.

20
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,563
So ask yourself: what is your company's late fee?
